result.reasoning:
Coverage of ICT, SMC, Wyckoff, AMT, price action, classic levels, sessions, regimes, PoT maths, calibration, UX and Pine engineering is deep: 22 final reports of 60-125 KB each, many with local replications. I grepped all *.final.md files plus skeptic.md for candidate gaps and found six areas that are missing or covered only in passing, each of which could plausibly change the engine or the trader's experience.

(1) Option-implied volatility as a forward-looking sigma for PoT. Nothing on VIX/VIX1D/VIX9D/DVOL as a sigma source, no expected-move bands, and no use of the IV term structure to detect event days. quant_regime dismissed implied vol in one line ("index-specific, monthly horizon"). quant_pot only notes that IV-based touch odds run high. Yet the study's main finding is that distance plus volatility explain almost all of which pool is hit next, and that the engine's sigma is time-blind. The event calendar is currently a manual text_area list (asset_specific).

(2) Derivatives positioning. Strike pinning and crypto liquidation heatmaps are covered. Dealer gamma regime (GEX, zero-gamma, call/put walls, vanna/charm, 0DTE), max pain, futures and crypto open interest, funding and basis, COT and short interest are absent (0 hits for COT, max pain, funding rate, Bessembinder, Barbon/Buraschi depth). So is ingesting external levels that Pine cannot compute.

(3) US market internals and breadth. 0 case-sensitive hits for USI:, $TICK, ADD, VOLD or TRIN. This is a new data channel for trend-day vs range-day and sweep hold vs reject on index futures, the main market for ICT traders.

(4) Validation on TradingView itself. No coverage of a strategy() companion with triple-barrier trades, Strategy Tester, Deep Backtesting, Bar Magnifier for same-bar double touches, Pine libraries to share the engine core, or pooled multi-symbol evaluation. The in-script ledger sees only 5k-40k chart bars and there is no offline compiler, so evaluation scale and honesty depend on this.

